germany's energy regulator has  temporarily stopped the approval process  for the nordstrom 2 gas pipeline it says  the operating company of the  controversial pipeline between russia  and germany needs to comply with german  law  it is yet another setback for the 10  billion euro project which has been  dogged by delays both ukraine and the  united states have opposed it russian  president vladimir putin said last month  the country could quickly boost natural  gas supplies to the eu  and rob watts from dw business has been  looking into this story for us hi rob so  what is behind this decision to halt the  approval process you mentioned there  that the regulator doesn't see this  company nordstrom 2 a.g is complying  with german law and that's not to say  that it's doing something wrong but the  point is that this is a russian-backed  company majority shareholders are a gas  gazprom the german they're the russian  company that's based in switzerland  which means it doesn't have to comply  generally day to day with german law so  what the regulators in germany want to  happen  is for nordstrom 2 to be transferred to  a company that is compliant with german  law now there's two ways you can do that  you can convert the existing company to  a german company or you can do what the  company is planning to do which is to  set up a subsidiary based in germany and  the regulators have said okay that  should solve the problem but we're only  going to restart this certification  process once we're satisfied that that  has been fully completed that all the  right assets are in the right hands and  that the hr so that the staff of this  company are being run by a german-based  company what are the consequences of  this  well for russia and germany this is bad  news because germany really wants to  start getting some more supplies of gas  i mean the whole of europe is suffering  from a gas shortage at the moment  they're seeing energy prices  skyrocketing the russians of course want  to start selling their gas to germany as  well so it can make extra money and it's  said that the second that nordstrom 2 is  permitted to con to to open  it can double the amount of gas that  it's sending here in fact the pipeline's  already full of gas they just have to  turn the taps on but that's not going to  happen until approval happens so does  anyone actually stand to benefit from  having this delay well this is  a hugely controversial project and there  are plenty of people who are against it  and some very big names we're talking  about the united states that hates  nordstrom 2 because it thinks that its  allies in europe are going to be too  reliant on russian gas as a as a  consequence of it and also you've got  ukraine which stands to lose out a great  deal of money on what i call transit  fees from the the gas that currently  passes through ukraine on its way to  western europe because it's going to go  through the bolt again it's going to  bypass ukraine so though they don't  necessarily stand to benefit from this  delay they won't be shedding a tear over  it all right very interesting rob watts  from dw business thank you very much for  bringing us that story
 

